  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    Twenty identical calls tell you the number wobbles. They do not tell you whether it means
    anything, so we ran {oursAmbiguity.clear.n} tickets a team lead would call obvious and
    {oursAmbiguity.ambiguous.n} we wrote on purpose so that two or three teams were all defensible,
    all against the same four options. The obvious ones came back at a mean confidence of
    {oursAmbiguity.clear.meanConfidence}, the arguable ones at {oursAmbiguity.ambiguous.meanConfidence}.
    The number is not decoration: it moves with how arguable the input is.
  </p>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    It is also not a probability you can read off, and we would rather say that than imply more than
    we measured. All {oursAmbiguity.clear.n + oursAmbiguity.ambiguous.n} answers were defensible, so
    there were no mistakes to calibrate against; whether a 0.7 means seventy per cent is still open.
    What the run does establish is the thing a caller needs: low confidence means "this item is
    genuinely arguable", which is exactly when a person should be looking.
  </p>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    We ran the same {oursAmbiguity.clear.n + oursAmbiguity.ambiguous.n} calls a second time, because a
    number measured once is a rumour. The means held: {oursAmbiguity.repeat.clearMeanConfidence} for
    the clear set again, {oursAmbiguity.repeat.ambiguousMeanConfidence} for the arguable one against
    {oursAmbiguity.ambiguous.meanConfidence}. The extremes did not — the widest answer in the arguable
    set read {oursAmbiguity.ambiguousSpread.max} in the first run and
    {oursAmbiguity.repeat.ambiguousSpread.max} in the second. So the direction is reliable and the
    value is not: gate on the band, not on the third decimal place, and expect the same ticket to
    land on a different side of a fine cut when you run it again.
  </p>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    One caveat that came out of the adversarial run and belongs here rather than in the fine print.
    The vendor's guarantee is about the shape of the answer, not about knowing when it has nothing to
    go on: sent an <em>empty</em> state, the model still answered, and answered at a confidence of
    {oursSchemaProbe.emptyState.confidence} ({oursSchemaProbe.emptyState.returned}). A confidence band
    is a policy for the answers you get, not a promise that the input was worth answering.
  </p>
