  <h3 id="decompose">How to use it: split the vague question</h3>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    The most common mistake is asking the model the same vague question a person would ask. "Is this
    email spam?" hides several judgements, and what comes back is a single number you can neither
    examine nor adjust. Ask five small questions instead — does it ask for a login credential; does
    it claim a prize nobody entered; does it manufacture urgency; does the sender domain match the
    brand it names; does the link text match where the link goes — then combine the five answers in
    your own code. Now the scoring rule is yours, and when it is wrong you can see which part was
    wrong.
  </p>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    The same discipline applies to any scale you define. Describe each level by the situation it
    describes, not by how strong it is: "broken, but there is a workaround" is answerable; "medium
    severity" is not, because medium has nothing to refer to. And write the levels out — the model
    does not read your 0, 1, 2 as an ordering.
  </p>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    That is the advice everywhere, so we tested it instead of repeating it. The same
    {oursPhrasing.items} reports were scored twice — once with the levels described by situation,
    once with the levels described only by how strong they are — and the two runs of numbers do not
    agree. Scores moved by a mean of {oursPhrasing.meanAbsDelta} of a level, and the ranking of the
    twelve changed for {oursPhrasing.orderAgreement.pairs - oursPhrasing.orderAgreement.agreed} of
    {oursPhrasing.orderAgreement.pairs} pairs, about one pair in seven. The widest single move was
    <em>{oursPhrasing.largestMove.item}</em>, which we would call a minor annoyance and which scored
    {oursPhrasing.largestMove.situational} against {oursPhrasing.largestMove.degree} — and
    {oursPhrasing.largestMoveRepeat.situational} against {oursPhrasing.largestMoveRepeat.degree} on a
    repeat, so even the size of the move is approximate. The report never changed; only the words
    under the numbers did.
  </p>
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); max-width: var(--measure);">
    For what this looks like in working code rather than in advice, see
    <a href="/jev-guide">the field notes on Jev</a> — eleven projects read first-hand, and the five
    teams in them independently drew the same line between what the model decides and what the
    program decides.
  </p>
